# Sheriff''s Deputy

**Alignments.** Any, but typically good or unprincipled. Most are honourable,
diligent, loyal to their sheriff, and live by the Code of the New West.

Deputies are the sheriff''s hand-picked officers and make up the bulk of any
large western police force. They are expected to be as loyal and trustworthy as
the sheriff, though they are rarely as skilled. Because they are always
volunteers they tend to be heroic and intensely loyal to the sheriff or the
town, sometimes to the point of fanaticism - there are plenty of stories of a
sheriff and five to ten good deputies holding a D-bee horde or an outlaw band
at bay when everything depended on it.

Deputies can also be family - a brother or a cousin - or old friends the lawman
trusts to handle themselves. At the other end, in bad situations, they may be
saloon bums, saddle tramps, petty crooks, farmers, or any able body willing to
stand alongside him. The quality of the law varies enormously from town to town.

## Numbers and pay

Most towns with a sheriff also have one to six deputies, and a large or
prosperous town or city may run an entire department of two to eight sheriffs
and ten to thirty deputies. But most western towns can barely afford a sheriff
at all - three in five have none - and will only have deputies if they can pay
them or the deputies will work for a pittance. The job usually offers fifty to
a hundred credits a week plus room and board.

## Lore

Also known simply as the Deputy, and as the Law Officer, the Lapdog of the Law
and the Little Tin Star. Most are dedicated to their sheriff and to the law, and
wear the tin star as a badge of honour.
